//! lotus/stereo_nyquist.rye — ALES128: stereo_nyquist_flip — the Nyquist flip of a master carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep, COMPLETING the stereo pure-rearrangement class. ALES107
//! named `nyquist_flip` — flip the sign of every ODD-indexed sample, y[n] = saturate((-1)^n · x[n]), holding the even seats fixed — for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10),
//! two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries the Nyquist flip into stereo the way
//! ALES127 carried invert: run ALES107's proven mono `nyquist_flip` on each channel; because both channels hold the same length, the parity pattern is IDENTICAL in each — the same even seats
//! fixed, the same odd seats negated — so modulating a master by the Nyquist frequency (the spectral-inversion trick that carries a lowpass shape to its highpass twin) lands in BOTH speakers at
//! once. With reverse (ALES125), rotate (ALES126), invert (ALES127), and now the parity flip lifted, the stereo suite's PURE REARRANGEMENT class stands whole. Like the other three it is TOTAL and
//! needs no validation. The spectral reading is interpretation, never computed here; the operation is a pure per-sample sign pattern in the time domain.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_nyquist_flip(sc) flips the odd-indexed samples of both channels, reusing ALES107's mono `nyquist_flip` per channel over its public API. Because mono nyquist_flip
//! is total (it names no span, so it raises no fault) and holds each channel's length, the balance invariant is preserved trivially — both channels enter equal-length, both flip their odd seats,
//! both hold their length, so they leave equal-length; there is no fault to forward, no EditError, no `try`. A stereo master is spectrally inverted as one thing, both speakers together.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO ALTERNATING-SIGN LAW: the left channel equals mono nyquist_flip(left) and the right equals mono nyquist_flip(right), each byte for byte against a hand-computed
//! vector, sample i holding saturate((-1)^i · x) per channel, even seats unchanged and odd seats negated. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their
//! starting length — the flip touches values on odd seats only, never the count, so the stereo image stays aligned. THE INVOLUTION LAW, WITH AN HONEST RAIL: stereo_nyquist_flip twice returns both
//! channels to the byte EXCEPT an odd-indexed sample_min (-32768) seat, which saturates to +32767 on the first flip and lands -32767 on the second per channel, the fixed two's-complement asymmetry
//! confined to the odd seats; even seats, never touched, always round-trip exactly on both channels. THE MAGNITUDE / PARITY LAW: off the rail |(-1)^i x| = |x|, so a channel whose peak is not
//! sample_min holds its peak magnitude (re-measured through ALES13); every even-indexed sample is a fixed point per channel, and an all-zero pair and an empty pair are each their own flip.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES128.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-025105_fill-ales128-lotus-stereo-nyquist.md
//! Stands on: lotus/nyquist.rye (ALES107) — the mono parity sign flip run once per channel, itself reusing ALES2's one true `saturate`; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips,
//! left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip and its buf/len/samples; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, used by the witness to prove a parity
//! sign-flip off the rail moves no loudness in either channel.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It flips the sign of existing odd-indexed samples in each channel and
//! fabricates none; it changes no length and reads no byte past either channel's len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker — the "Nyquist frequency"
//! it names is the interpretation of a pure per-sample sign pattern, not a measured rate.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_nyquist.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const nyquist = @import("nyquist.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_nyquist_version = "20260815.025105";

/// Flip the sign of every odd-indexed sample of both channels of `sc` in lockstep, holding the even seats fixed, the stereo image staying balanced and each channel's length holding. Because both
/// channels hold the same length, the parity pattern is identical in each. This is y[n] = saturate((-1)^n · x[n]) per channel — modulation by the Nyquist frequency. Total on any balanced
/// StereoClip: mono nyquist_flip raises no fault and holds each length, so the two channels leave equal-length and no refusal can land between them. Reuses ALES107's mono nyquist_flip per channel.
pub fn stereo_nyquist_flip(sc: *pan.StereoClip) void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // Mono nyquist_flip is total and holds the length, so each channel flips its odd seats and no fault can land between the two edits.
    nyquist.nyquist_flip(&sc.left);
    nyquist.nyquist_flip(&sc.right);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — each held its length under the same parity flip, so the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO ALTERNATING-SIGN LAW — each channel equals ALES107's mono nyquist_flip on that channel byte for byte, sample i holding saturate((-1)^i · x); THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW —
//     left.len == right.len after, both holding their starting length; THE INVOLUTION LAW WITH AN HONEST RAIL — twice the identity off the floor, an odd-indexed sample_min pinning to -32767 on
//     both channels while even seats always round-trip; THE MAGNITUDE / PARITY LAW — each channel's peak unchanged off the rail, every even seat a fixed point, an all-zero and empty pair their own flip ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

/// A channel's current peak magnitude, re-measured through ALES13 — the reader the magnitude law checks each channel against.
fn clip_peak(clip: *const timeline.Clip) !u32 {
    const m = try meter.measure(clip);
    return meter.peak(&m);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO ALTERNATING-SIGN LAW — each channel equals ALES107's mono nyquist_flip on that channel: even seats held, odd seats negated. Proven side by side over one anchor with different
    //    per-channel samples; an odd-indexed floor rides along on the left so its saturated negation (+32768 clamps to sample_max) is proven in place.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, -32768, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&sc);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, -32768, 50 });
        nyquist.nyquist_flip(&ml);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        nyquist.nyquist_flip(&mr);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: seats 0,2,4 held, seats 1,3 negated; the odd-indexed floor -32768 saturates to +32767 on the left.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, -20, 30, 32767, 50 })) return error.LeftFlipWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, -21, 31, -41, 51 })) return error.RightFlipWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; the stereo image stays aligned. Proven at an even and an odd length.
    {
        var even = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&even, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7, 8 });
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&even);
        if (even.left.len != even.right.len) return error.EvenUnbalanced;
        if (even.left.len != 4) return error.EvenLength;
        if (!eq(even.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, -2, 3, -4 })) return error.EvenLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(even.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7, -8 })) return error.EvenRightWrong;

        var odd = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&odd, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&odd);
        if (odd.left.len != odd.right.len) return error.OddUnbalanced;
        if (!eq(odd.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, -2, 3 })) return error.OddLeftWrong; // seats 0,2 held
        if (!eq(odd.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 6, -7, 8 })) return error.OddRightWrong;
    }

    // 3a. THE INVOLUTION LAW, off the rail — for a pair free of an odd-indexed sample_min, stereo_nyquist_flip twice returns both channels to the byte: the flip is its own inverse.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        const l0 = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, -40, 32767, 6 };
        const r0 = [_]i16{ -11, 22, -33, 44, -32767, 7 };
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&sc);
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&sc);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0)) return error.InvolutionLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.InvolutionRightWrong;
    }

    // 3b. THE INVOLUTION LAW, on the rail — an odd-indexed sample_min does NOT round-trip: -32768 → 32767 → -32767, confined to the odd seats; every even seat (never touched) round-trips exactly.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 500, -32768, 500, -1 }, &[_]i16{ -32768, 9, -32768, -32768 });
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&sc);
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&sc);
        // Left: only seat 1 is an odd-indexed floor → -32767; seats 0,2,3 round-trip (seat 3 is odd but -1 negates cleanly). Right: seat 0 is even (a floor, never touched, exact), seat 2 even
        // (exact), seat 3 odd-indexed floor → -32767; seat 1 (9) round-trips.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 500, -32767, 500, -1 })) return error.RailLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -32768, 9, -32768, -32767 })) return error.RailRightWrong;
    }

    // 4a. THE MAGNITUDE LAW — off the rail |(-1)^i x| = |x|, so each channel whose peak is not sample_min holds its peak magnitude re-measured through ALES13; the modulation reflects the spectrum,
    //     never the loudness. Peaks 30000 and 32000, both off the floor.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -30000, 5, -5, 30000 }, &[_]i16{ 50, -32000, 70, 5 });
        const lb = try clip_peak(&sc.left);
        const rb = try clip_peak(&sc.right);
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&sc);
        if (try clip_peak(&sc.left) != lb) return error.LeftPeakMoved;
        if (try clip_peak(&sc.right) != rb) return error.RightPeakMoved;
    }

    // 4b. THE PARITY / FIXPOINT LAW — every even-indexed sample is a fixed point, and an all-zero pair and an empty pair are each their own flip.
    {
        var empty = pan.StereoClip{};
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&empty);
        if (empty.left.len != 0 or empty.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyChanged;

        var zeros = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&zeros, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 });
        stereo_nyquist_flip(&zeros);
        if (!eq(zeros.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.ZerosLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(zeros.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.ZerosRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-nyquist: STEREO_NYQUIST_FLIP — the Nyquist flip of a master, both channels in lockstep, completing the stereo pure-rearrangement class. Flip every odd-indexed sample " ++
        "of both channels, reusing ALES107's mono nyquist_flip per channel; because both channels hold the same length the parity pattern is identical in each, so modulating a master by the " ++
        "Nyquist frequency (the spectral-inversion trick carrying a lowpass shape to its highpass twin) lands in both speakers at once. With reverse, rotate, invert, and now the parity flip " ++
        "lifted, the class stands whole; like the other three it is total and needs no validation. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO ALTERNATING-SIGN LAW: each channel equals ALES107's mono " ++
        "nyquist_flip on that channel byte for byte, sample i holding saturate((-1)^i · x), even seats held and odd seats negated. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit " ++
        "and both hold their starting length, the stereo image staying aligned. THE INVOLUTION LAW, WITH AN HONEST RAIL: stereo_nyquist_flip twice returns both channels to the byte except an " ++
        "odd-indexed sample_min (-32768), which saturates to +32767 then lands -32767 per channel while even seats always round-trip. THE MAGNITUDE / PARITY LAW: off the rail each channel's peak " ++
        "re-measured through ALES13 is unchanged, every even seat a fixed point, an all-zero pair and an empty pair each their own flip. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips parity-flipped " ++
        "through the proven mono nyquist_flip, reading no byte past either channel's len; the Nyquist frequency named is interpretation of a sign pattern, not a measured rate; no network, no " ++
        "keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
